# How the Matrix fly works

**Import a biological connectome. Run it as a Cadence patch net. Feed in sensory
data and read out neural decisions. This example focuses on getting that loop
running quickly with the current Cadence library and its browser
implementation. It does not yet reproduce every detail of neural activity or
the body: precise spike timing, the full set of neurotransmitters and their
receptor effects, slow chemical signaling, biological hunger regulation and
individual muscle dynamics are examples of what remains simplified or absent.
The demo will develop further as Cadence gains capabilities and the model gains
better biological data and validated sensory and body interfaces.**

Importing biological connectomes is one application of Cadence, rather than its
main purpose. Cadence is a general library for computation and learning through
local repair and settlement, including the research toward deep, recursively
observing networks. A nervous system's recurrent wiring fits its local-state,
read-port and feedback representation: this example instantiates that wiring
as a settlement model. The idea that animal brains themselves compute through
shared equilibria motivates the work; the import alone does not establish that
biological hypothesis.

**The brain chooses goals. The body controller carries them out.**

The simulation runs every neuron in the full retained brain-and-nerve-cord
connectome. It receives simulated versions of the sensory channels a fly uses:
eye pixels, odors, balance, touch and taste. These enter a Cadence patch net;
they are not a recording or exact reconstruction of a living fly's sensory input. The network settles, compares
banana and bread, and supplies an approach-or-avoid choice. Its mushroom-body
connections can learn from what happens after that choice is executed.

The body controller makes that choice visible: it flies toward the selected
fruit or retreats from it, lands, rests, feeds, cleans itself and takes off.
Routes and body routines are supplied mechanics. The neural decision is which
fruit to consider and whether to approach or avoid it; cleaning is currently a
body routine after landing, not a separately learned neural decision.

## 1. From connectome to patch-net brain

The model contains **150,802 neurons and 1,877,099 directed connection classes**
from the retained [BANC brain-and-nerve-cord](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-026-10735-w)
fixture. Every neuron and edge in
that fixture is included. The importer keeps proofread neurons and
generally requires five contacts per connection; all KC→MBON connections are
retained even below that threshold.

Each neuron becomes a patch with a local potential and activity. Its incoming
connections are read ports: they bring in activities published by other neurons.
Contact counts, predicted transmitter signs and supplied physiological gains
set the initial interactions. The mushroom-body learning connections and output
biases receive a calibrated starting point.
In particular, KC→MBON efficacies are normalized so connection classes start
with equal effective magnitude instead of their measured relative contact
counts. This is a supplied initialization for the learning demonstration;
it does not preserve all of the specimen's connection strengths or establish
which of those strengths were innate versus learned.

Including every retained neuron preserves the wiring; it does not recover every
biological mechanism. The export supplies a graded neuron model and operating
points, and does not reproduce peptide, hormonal or volume signaling. A settled
state satisfies this model's equations. Whether it produces appropriate fly
behavior is a separate check.

The current transmitter mapping is deliberately limited:

| Mechanism | What this model implements |
| --- | --- |
| Fast chemical connections | Predicted acetylcholine becomes a positive weight; GABA, glutamate and histamine become negative weights. These are supplied sign conventions, without postsynaptic receptor types. |
| Dopamine and other modulators | The importer gives dopamine, octopamine, serotonin and tyramine zero fast-synapse sign. Their release, receptor effects and slow modulation are not separately implemented. |
| Reward-dependent learning | A supplied critic computes a dopamine-like scalar that multiplies local KC→MBON traces. Activity in the imported PAM/PPL1 neurons does not compute that scalar. |
| Adaptation | Cadence's Python neuron model supports a local adaptation state; this browser model disables it. Adaptation alone would not supply hunger physiology. |

See the explicit [transmitter mapping](../fruitfly/banc.py) and
[learning multiplier](../web/learner.js). A zero sign here means that this
model omits the transmitter's action, not that the biological transmitter has
no effect. Representing those effects requires declared physiological
assumptions and tested coupling to neural state. That may be achievable through
the existing patch rule; a separate biochemical engine has not been shown
necessary. Changing all zero signs to positive weights would not supply the
missing physiology.

**Local repair is how the brain computes.** Each patch adjusts its potential
toward its combined incoming activity, sensory drive and bias. That adjustment
changes what connected patches read next. Shared inputs make the local
neighborhoods overlap; recurrent connections remain intact. The connectome is
not flattened into a feed-forward network.

The browser repeats these local updates until the largest equation mismatch
across **all neurons** is at most `1e-6`, with a live-demo cap of 256 rounds per phase. Only a
qualified settlement can supply a new goal. Failed attempts are reported and
the previous neural state is retained; an unfinished solve never becomes a
successful choice. This is a numerical check for that input, not a guarantee
that every possible input converges.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    W[World and fly body] --> I[Eye pixels, odor, balance, touch and taste]
    I --> P[Cadence patches: local repairs through recurrent connections]
    P --> Q[Check whole-network settlement]
    Q --> C[Neural choice: fruit + approach or avoid]
    C --> B[Body controller: route, wings, landing and body routines]
    B --> W
    W --> O[Actual outcome]
    O --> L[Local mushroom-body learning]
    L --> P
```

[export_web.py](../tools/export_web.py) builds the model;
[brain.js](../web/brain.js) implements the patch dynamics.

## 2. What goes into the patch net

| Sensory input | What the brain receives |
| --- | --- |
| Vision | Actual raw 64×32 head-camera RGB pixels, converted to brightness drives for all 1,831 retained photoreceptors. |
| Smell | Banana and yeast odor plumes sampled on the two sides of the fly, with hunger-dependent gain. |
| Balance and motion | Body orientation, rotation, airspeed and flight-state signals encoded into named sensory populations. |
| Touch and taste | Contact with the environment and sugar contact encoded into sensory drives. |
| Outcome context | Brief reward-related PAM/PPL1 population drives after outcomes. A separate reward/critic helper supplies the learning multiplier. |

The eye inset shows the same camera projection with cosmetic facets and tint;
the neural input uses a separate raw capture without those display effects.
Hiding the inset does not turn off vision. Pixel-to-photoreceptor mapping is an
engineered grid because anatomical viewing directions are unavailable.
Balance inputs also use supplied encoders; not all visual processing comes
through the retinal pixels.

**Input clamping means holding the sensory drives steady for one solve.** It
does not force sensory neurons to a prescribed activity. Those neurons still
receive recurrent signals and settle with the rest of the network. The next
observation supplies new drives and starts the next settlement.

The nervous-system scan replays actual recorded activity and local potential
repairs from the solve. It is a view of numerical neural dynamics, not invented
flashes or biological spike timing. The optional settling inspector exposes
individual patches, shared read ports and residuals.

While a solve runs, the scan streams its actual activity and last-update repairs,
with a fixed color scale and the phase, step and residual shown. These are
intermediate states, including attempts that may fail. Candidate comparisons
and positive/negative nudges are separate solves; switching between them is not
a biological propagation-time measurement. Progress never grants motor or
learning authority.

See [eye.js](../web/eye.js), [retina.js](../web/retina.js) and
[worker.js](../web/worker.js) for the sensory input path.

## 3. What the fly decides and how it learns

**Innate appetite comes before learning.** A fly should not need a sugar lesson
to acquire the basic drive to seek and eat food. In this demo, hunger currently
enables food-choice requests and changes odor sensitivity; it is not yet a
validated appetite circuit. The full-connectome model can settle and still
choose to avoid food while hungry. Local learning tests do not establish that
this missing innate behavior works.

Biological hunger can alter olfactory processing through neuromodulation, as
shown by [Root et al.](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3073827/).
That motivates modeling the sensory physiology, but does not validate the
demo's supplied gains or establish that wiring alone specifies the dynamics.
Learning here concerns experience-dependent odor preferences, not teaching
the animal that it needs to eat.

**Which fruit?** The network settles under each odor context from the same
starting state and observation. The supplied attention protocol masks the
competing odor during each probe. Settled mushroom-body output scores determine
the target probabilities; a sampler draws the fruit. No fruit coordinates or
explicit sugar-location label enter these comparisons, although sugar can be
visible in the eye pixels. A near tie means weak preference, so either fruit
may be selected. The console shows both target probabilities.

Banana starts with sugar on it; that is the reward location, not a built-in
instruction to prefer banana. The initial distant odors are very weak, and
the first target odds are approximately 50/50. Bread is therefore a valid
exploratory choice. `/reset` also resets learned preferences.

**Approach or avoid?** The network settles under the selected odor. Its MBON
outputs supply the two action probabilities. The body controller receives
that selected fruit and action—it does not choose the strongest smell or
invent a random destination while waiting.

**What changes with experience?** The learning mechanism uses the same patch
dynamics with positive and negative output nudges. Each plastic KC→MBON
connection reads the contrast between its endpoints' joint activities in those
two settlements. The free state and both perturbed states must qualify before
eligibility is stored. The selected target probe supplies the action's free
state, whose whole-network residual is checked again before either nudge.
An actual, matching execution and outcome are required before that choice can
receive credit. A rejected phase cancels that search and permits a new request;
it cannot leave the fly waiting forever on a discarded decision.
Each goal request certifies its own sensory input and phases, so it can run even
if a preceding unattended observation failed. That does not admit failed phases
or grant permission to use unqualified motor readings.

A supplied critic converts the outcome into a reward error that scales these
local traces. **16,795 KC→MBON connection classes** can change; other connections
stay fixed during a run. Updated weights affect later settlements and choices.
The console reports how many weights actually moved. A zero-change update is
not presented as learned improvement.

Sugar gives reward `+1`; an empty fruit gives `0`; a matching aversive event
gives `-1`. With a new critic predicting zero, an empty-food visit can correctly
produce no update. Learning is stronger when the actual odor activates the
mushroom body: a faint, distant choice can leave very little eligibility.
Only the sugar-bearing food supplies calories in this two-choice lesson. An
unsugared bread visit therefore cannot reduce hunger; the food model does not
represent bread's ordinary nutritional content.
The current checks establish local odor-linked learning, not a reliable learned
banana preference throughout the room.

This is learning of odor-linked approach/avoid responses. It does not establish
learned flight trajectories, learned grooming or an exact-gradient guarantee
for the asymmetric connectome. See [learner.js](../web/learner.js).

The [browser check](../receipts/browser_goal_responsive_learning_2026-09-27.json)
follows a real neural choice through landing, sugar reward and nonzero updates.
A separate [controlled learning check](../tools/check_goal_learning.mjs) changes
synapses with one synthetic lesson, then restores only those weights to verify
that they caused the changed neural response.
A [paired food check](../receipts/food_learning_paired_2026-09-27.json) uses
actual nearby odor encodings with explicit synthetic rewards and frozen-weight
controls. Rewarding either food changes its own nearby approach response more
than the other food's response. It uses the offline 1,024-round budget without
retinal input; it does not establish learned remote food selection in the demo.

## 4. Why the fly has a body controller

The brain's checked neural choice supplies a goal; the simulation translates
that goal into body movement. It does not simulate each motor-neuron spike,
neuromuscular junction, muscle contraction, joint force or wingbeat interaction.
Those details are coarse-grained into a body controller so the imported network
can interact with a visible world at browser speed. The connectome also does not specify all the receptor,
neuromuscular and muscle dynamics needed to do that. This model uses graded
neural activities and equilibrium solves, with a practical motor interface.

Think of it as a fly brain in an Iron Man suit: the neural goal drives a supplied
movement system. That is a metaphor for the interface, not a claim about what
the model experiences. The suit also supplies routines and wall clearance;
those movements are not new decisions recovered from the connectome.

[goal-life.js](../web/goal-life.js) implements that interface:

- **Approach** turns the brain-selected fruit into a route, aligns the body,
  supplies wing commands and descends to land when nearby.
- **Avoid** executes a finite retreat from the selected fruit, bounded inside
  the room, then asks the brain for another choice.
- **After landing**, fixed body routines provide resting, feeding if hungry
  and on sugar, cleaning the head, legs or wings, and takeoff.
- **At startup**, a one-time body launch follows the initial heading at up to
  `0.30 m/s`, for at most `1.2 m` or `6 s`, shortened near walls. It selects no
  fruit and earns no learning credit, then joins the ordinary waiting cruise.
  A qualified neural goal takes over; pausing or changing the scene cancels
  the initial launch.
- **Between neural replies**, the body continues the accepted goal smoothly.
  The bounded travel window starts after admission, so computation time does
  not cancel a landing halfway through. Without a goal, it cruises along its
  current heading and makes supplied room-clearance turns. This movement
  chooses no food and receives no learning credit. Fresh, qualified motor-neuron readouts can
  add bounded wing steering trim, subject to their one-second freshness limit.
- **A nudge** releases any perch, lets the actual impulse move the body for
  `0.15 s`, then resumes supplied stabilization and cruise. This physical
  recovery is distinct from a neural escape choice. Only a previously executed,
  matching food choice can receive the aversive learning outcome.

The body moves while the serial worker computes. Goal replies have a bounded
`20 s` admission window, and checked observations can enable a new goal request
for `10 s`; neither turns an old observation into fresh motor steering. Scene
changes withdraw old choices. The worker finishes its current job before the
newest observation is submitted, preventing repeated nudges from building a
queue of obsolete solves.

**Hunger stays between zero and one.** It rises while the fly is awake and
falls only while it is physically supported on the currently sugared fruit
and feeding. Satiety, removal of sugar or loss of food contact ends feeding.
Hunger also changes odor sensitivity and whether another food decision is
requested. These are supplied body physiology rules with compressed demo
timing, rather than a claim to recover biological metabolism from anatomy.

The supplied cruise target is **0.30 m/s**, and time defaults to **1×**. Wall
clearance, stabilization and body routines are assistance. They do not earn
separate credit for learned obstacle avoidance or learned motor coordination.
The body controller is also needed because reliable direct connectome control
of those movements has not yet been established; browser cost is not the only
reason for the abstraction.

There is **one demo controller**, with the Matrix console, nervous-system scan
and fly's-eye view. `/status` shows brain/body readings; `/paths` shows the
neural choice beside the measured route. `/sugar banana`, `/sugar bread` and
`/nudge` change the fly's experience. `/pause`, `/resume` and `/reset` control
the demonstration. Display options change the view, never who controls the fly.

The [README](../README.md#run) links the checks and their recorded evidence for
the sensory, settlement, decision and learning mechanisms. The useful starting
point is already tangible: imported wiring receives data, patches repair their
states, checked outputs guide a body, and outcomes can change local synapses.
You can inspect that loop now and test richer models as the example develops.
